Let me construct this field guide directly from my thorough analysis of all 22 papers. The analysis covers innovation patterns, reviewer behavior, gaps, combinations, and strategic advice.

```json
{
  "innovation_patterns": [
    {
      "pattern": "Reframe the budget accounting",
      "description": "Reveal that prior comparisons were unfair by introducing a new cost variable or showing an existing one was ignored, then re-derive optimal strategies under the corrected accounting. The contribution is the framework itself, not necessarily a new algorithm.",
      "examples": ["Singhi et al. (COLM 2025) — showed SC vs GenRM comparisons were unfair when verification token cost was unaccounted", "VG-Search (NeurIPS 2025) — revealed verification granularity g as a continuous tunable parameter between beam search and BoN that no one was optimizing", "OSCA (NAACL 2025) — showed single-configuration allocation was suboptimal and mixed allocation is provably convex"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Eliminate an expensive component while preserving function",
      "description": "Remove a costly module (reward model, human annotations, large training set) by exploiting an overlooked cheaper signal that achieves similar discriminative power. Papers following this pattern achieve dramatic resource reduction ratios (80-99%) that make for compelling narratives.",
      "examples": ["ST-BoN (NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight) — removes external reward model entirely using CoE hidden-state consistency, saving >80% KV-cache", "ThinkPRM (arXiv 2025) — reduces PRM training data by 99% (1K vs 712K examples) by leveraging base model reasoning ability", "RL^V (DeepMind 2025) — gets a free verifier as byproduct of RL training data with zero additional GPU allocation", "DeepSeek-R1 (Nature 2025) — removes human step-level annotations via pure RL with rule-based rewards"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Recast discriminative tasks as generative",
