  "untried_combinations": [
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Lagrangian decomposition (from Adaptive Constrained) extended to the 2D (K, d) decision space, using the bilinear cost structure K*(c_g + c_v(d)) to decompose the budget constraint. For fixed dual variable λ, each instance independently selects (K*, d*) = argmax [P_correct(K,d) - λ*K*(c_g + c_v(d))]. The clean decomposition noted in the theoretical anchor — for fixed d, optimal K is deterministic — reduces the 2D problem to searching over d, making binary search over λ still feasible.",
      "from_papers": ["Adaptive Constrained (arXiv 2026)", "Singhi et al. (COLM 2025)"],
      "why_promising": "The Lagrangian paper already solved the K-only case with formal regret bounds. The bilinear cost structure means extending to (K,d) doesn't break the decomposition — d is a discrete variable with only 4 levels (none/ORM/PRM/GenRM), so exhaustive search over d per instance is tractable. This gives provable approximation guarantees our proposed paper needs."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "ST-BoN's Chain-of-Embedding (CoE) hidden-state consistency as a fast difficulty signal feeding into verification depth selection: compute CoE over the first few dozen tokens of N parallel candidates, then use the consistency score not just for pruning (as ST-BoN does) but as a continuous difficulty estimator that selects verification depth d. High consistency → skip verification (d=none); medium → ORM; low → PRM; very low + high entropy → GenRM.",
      "from_papers": ["ST-BoN (NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight)", "Oops Wait (arXiv 2026)", "VG-Search (NeurIPS 2025)"],
      "why_promising": "ST-BoN's CoE is already computed as part of parallel generation with near-zero marginal cost, making it an ideal online difficulty signal. Combined with Oops Wait's entropy/hesitation markers, this creates a multi-feature difficulty estimator that doesn't require any pre-computation (unlike Snell et al.'s 2048 samples) or offline profiling (unlike VG-Search's accuracy curves). ST-BoN explicitly leaves open the extension to 'variable-depth verification selection.'"
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "DiffAdapt's prefill MLP probe extended from 3 generation-strategy bins to N verification-depth levels: train the same lightweight frozen-hidden-state probe to predict not just difficulty category but optimal verification depth, using the bilinear decomposition's oracle labels instead of entropy/correctness thresholds.",
      "from_papers": ["DiffAdapt (ICLR 2026)", "Adaptive Constrained (arXiv 2026)", "Singhi et al. (COLM 2025)"],
      "why_promising": "DiffAdapt proved prefill probes work for difficulty classification (accepted at ICLR 2026 despite initial low scores). The Adaptive Constrained paper showed imitation learning from oracle allocation is effective (0.4-1.4pp gap). Combining both: use oracle (K*,d*) labels from Lagrangian optimization as supervision for DiffAdapt-style probes, getting fine-grained per-instance routing instead of 3 coarse bins."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "RL^V's free-verifier-from-RL-data approach extended to train a hierarchy of verifiers (ORM + PRM + GenRM) simultaneously during RL training, with a learned router that selects which verifier to invoke per instance at inference. Training cost is amortized since all verifier supervision comes from RL rollouts.",
      "from_papers": ["RL^V (DeepMind 2025)", "GenPRM (AAAI 2026)", "PAV/Rewarding Progress (ICLR 2025 Spotlight)"],
      "why_promising": "RL^V showed verifier training is essentially free when piggybacking on RL data. GenPRM showed generative verification can be trained efficiently. PAV showed advantages are better than Q-values for process verification. Combining all three yields a model that generates solutions AND can self-verify at multiple depths, with a learned router selecting the cheapest sufficient depth per instance."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "DORA's direction-level allocation within generation combined with per-direction verification depth selection: after generating K candidates and clustering into semantic directions, apply different verification depths to different directions based on their diversity and PRM-score variance, rather than applying uniform verification to all candidates.",
      "from_papers": ["DORA (NeurIPS 2025)", "VG-Search (NeurIPS 2025)", "When Does Verification Pay Off (arXiv 2025)"],
      "why_promising": "DORA proved that treating all candidates equally is suboptimal. 'When Does Verification Pay Off' showed verification value varies by problem characteristics. Extending this to vary verification depth by candidate cluster — not just by problem — is a natural two-level allocation hierarchy that no paper has explored."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Process Advantage Verifiers (PAV) as an online difficulty estimator: use the advantage signal A^μ from early reasoning steps as a real-time difficulty proxy (low-advantage early steps indicate the problem is hard and the model is not making progress), triggering escalation to deeper verification for subsequent steps.",
      "from_papers": ["PAV/Rewarding Progress (ICLR 2025 Spotlight)", "Oops Wait (arXiv 2026)", "Snell et al. (ICLR 2025 Oral)"],
      "why_promising": "PAV advantages are already being computed if process verification is used, making them a zero-marginal-cost difficulty signal. Unlike Snell et al.'s offline difficulty estimation, PAV advantages are available in real-time during generation. Low early-step advantages could trigger escalation from step-level to GenRM verification, creating a natural difficulty-adaptive verification depth scheduler."
    }
  ],

  "method_building_blocks": [
    {
      "tool": "Chain-of-Embedding (CoE) consistency signal",
      "what_it_does": "Measures pairwise consistency of N parallel candidates' hidden-state trajectories across transformer layers using L2 + angular distance. Produces a continuous similarity score from partial generations (first few dozen tokens) without requiring full sequence completion or external reward models.",
      "used_by": ["ST-BoN (NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Online difficulty estimator for verification depth selection — high CoE consistency indicates easy problems where verification can be skipped, low consistency indicates hard problems requiring deep verification. Near-zero marginal cost since it's computed during parallel generation."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Lagrangian budget decomposition with binary search over dual variable",
      "what_it_does": "Converts a globally-coupled budget constraint into independent per-instance subproblems via a single dual variable λ ('unit price of compute'). A cost-monotonicity theorem guarantees binary search over λ matches any target budget exactly. Formal regret bounds tie suboptimality to imitation error.",
      "used_by": ["Adaptive Constrained (arXiv 2026)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Joint (K, d) optimization where the bilinear cost structure K*(c_g + c_v(d)) preserves the decomposition. For each candidate λ, each instance independently selects optimal (K*, d*), making the 2D extension tractable."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Prefill hidden-state MLP probe for difficulty classification",
      "what_it_does": "A frozen lightweight MLP classifier on the LLM's final prefill hidden state that predicts problem difficulty category. Trained on ~900 self-supervised examples using entropy/correctness thresholds. Requires no model retraining and adds negligible inference latency.",
      "used_by": ["DiffAdapt (ICLR 2026 Poster)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Verification depth routing with finer granularity than DiffAdapt's 3 bins. Could be trained via imitation learning on Lagrangian oracle labels to predict optimal (K*, d*) tuples directly."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Generative binary verification via P(Yes|x,y,prompt)",
      "what_it_does": "Casts solution verification as a next-token prediction task. The verifier score is p(Yes)/(p(Yes)+p(No)) after optionally generating a CoT reasoning trace. Can be scaled at inference via majority voting over N independent verification paths.",
      "used_by": ["GenRM (ICLR 2025)", "RL^V (DeepMind 2025)", "GenPRM (AAAI 2026)", "ThinkPRM (arXiv 2025)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "The maximum-depth verification tier in a multi-depth framework. Its variable compute cost (0 tokens for no-CoT to 8192 tokens for full ThinkPRM) makes it naturally parameterizable as a continuous depth knob."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Process Advantage Verifiers (PAV) with prover-based advantages",
      "what_it_does": "Computes step-level advantages A^μ = Q^μ - V^μ under a separate complementary prover policy (Best-of-K), measuring reasoning progress rather than state value. Moderate-strength provers (K=4) optimally balance distinguishability and alignment.",
      "used_by": ["Rewarding Progress / PAV (ICLR 2025 Spotlight)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Mid-depth verification tier (between ORM and GenRM) in the multi-depth framework, and as a real-time difficulty signal — low early-step advantages indicate the problem is hard and may warrant escalation to deeper verification."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Hesitation/entropy token-level signals",
      "what_it_does": "Categorizes generation tokens into hesitation markers ('wait', 'hmm'), logical connectives ('therefore', 'thus'), and entropy-based confidence signals. Their probability mass at specific positions strongly predicts final answer correctness across models and benchmarks.",
      "used_by": ["Oops Wait (arXiv 2026)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Cheap feature inputs for the difficulty estimator in per-instance verification depth routing. These signals are available from the first few reasoning tokens and require only monitoring the model's own token probabilities — no external components needed."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Verification granularity parameter g",
      "what_it_does": "A continuous knob that interpolates between per-step beam search (g=1) and end-only Best-of-N (g=L) by setting the number of generation steps between verifier calls. Comes with a compute cost model C_total decomposing generation and verification FLOPs.",
      "used_by": ["VG-Search (NeurIPS 2025)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "An additional dimension in the joint optimization framework — beyond selecting verification depth d, also selecting verification frequency g per-instance, creating a (K, d, g) allocation space."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Verifier gain metric and cross-family verification principle",
      "what_it_does": "Verifier gain = Precision(Solver,Verifier;Dataset) - SolverAcc(Solver;Dataset) measures the accuracy improvement from adding verification. The cross-family principle states that verifiers from different model families consistently outperform self-verification or intra-family verification.",
      "used_by": ["When Does Verification Pay Off (arXiv 2025)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Instance-level verifier selection: if cross-family verification is better on average, per-instance routing could further exploit this by selecting which verifier family to use based on problem characteristics, maximizing verifier gain per compute unit."
    },
    {
      "tool": "GRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization)",
      "what_it_does": "Critic-free RL algorithm computing group-relative advantages across G sampled outputs with only rule-based rewards (correctness + format). Induces spontaneous emergence of self-verification, reflection, and dynamic reasoning strategies in sufficiently capable models.",
      "used_by": ["DeepSeek-R1 (Nature 2025)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Training the base generator in any generation-verification framework. The RL rollouts produced during GRPO training are exactly the (problem, solution, correctness) tuples that RL^V showed can supervise a free verifier."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Direction-level semantic clustering with uniqueness reweighting",
      "what_it_does": "Clusters candidate solutions into semantic directions via embedding cosine similarity, computes uniqueness scores from softmax affinity matrices, and reweights PRM scores to correct the provable bias of solution-level allocation toward overrepresented directions.",
      "used_by": ["DORA (NeurIPS 2025)"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Within-generation allocation in the K-d framework: after generating K candidates, cluster them and apply DORA's correction before verification, ensuring verification compute isn't wasted on redundant solution directions."
    }
  ],

  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Math-only evaluation is a paper-killer at top venues: GenPRM, PAV, DORA, Inference Scaling Laws, and OSCA are all flagged for this limitation. DiffAdapt's 8-benchmark suite spanning math/science/MMLU was cited positively. Include ≥2 domains (math + code minimum, ideally + science) or expect Reject.",
    "Unfair compute comparisons will be caught immediately: Singhi et al.'s entire COLM contribution was exposing that prior SC-vs-GenRM comparisons didn't account for verification token cost. Any paper in this space that doesn't meticulously equalize total FLOPs — including verifier inference, embedding computation, and routing overhead — will be rejected on methodological grounds alone.",
    "Instance-level allocation can fail: OSCA explicitly reports that k-NN instance-level allocation performed worse than domain-level uniform allocation. DiffAdapt's 3-bin approach captures only ~45% of oracle savings. Naive per-instance routing (nearest-neighbor on features, simple regression) is a proven failure mode. The difficulty estimator must be carefully designed and ablated.",
    "The baseline fear is real — simple heuristics are strong: Before building a complex learned allocator, test trivially simple baselines: 'always use PRM for problems with >N tokens,' 'use GenRM only if SC entropy exceeds threshold,' or DiffAdapt's 3-bin approach. If these capture >80% of the learned method's gain, the paper's contribution collapses. Multiple papers (DiffAdapt's initial 4/4/4/6 scores, OSCA's k-NN failure) show reviewers actively check this.",
    "Ignoring verification overhead in cost models: Inference Scaling Laws and multiple tree search papers were flagged for not including PRM inference cost in compute budgets. VG-Search had to build an explicit cost model C_total decomposing generation and verification FLOPs. Always include the router/estimator's own cost in the budget.",
    "Assuming verification always helps: Singhi et al. proved SC beats GenRM for most practical budgets. 'When Does Verification Pay Off' showed reasoning post-training paradoxically weakens self-verification (73-96% of outputs already self-verify). The framework must gracefully handle the 'no verification' option and demonstrate it's selected appropriately for easy instances.",
    "Overfitting difficulty estimation to one model or domain: DiffAdapt's prefill probes don't transfer across architectures. Snell et al.'s difficulty quintiles were validated only on MATH with PaLM 2. Oops Wait notes hesitation signal generalization across non-English languages and non-mathematical domains is untested. Train and evaluate difficulty estimators across ≥2 model families.",
    "Conflating correlation with causation in difficulty signals: Oops Wait explicitly flags that hesitation tokens 'may reflect rather than drive reasoning success.' Building a routing system on correlated-but-non-causal signals risks Goodhart's Law when the model or task distribution shifts. Validate difficulty signal robustness on held-out distributions.",
    "Withdrawn/controversial papers as building blocks require caution: ThinkPRM was withdrawn from ICLR 2026 for unclear reasons. Building on withdrawn work without independent validation of their claims is risky. If using generative PRM approaches as a depth tier, validate independently rather than citing withdrawn numbers.",
    "Over-engineering the routing mechanism at the expense of the verification hierarchy: The field already has multiple smart routing methods (DiffAdapt probes, CoE signals, Lagrangian decomposition). The under-explored axis is the verification depth continuum itself — what exactly are the tiers, what are their cost-accuracy curves, and how do they compose? Don't spend 80% of the paper on routing and 20% on defining what's being routed to."
  ],

  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions that will likely define 2026-2027 research. First, per-instance adaptive compute allocation is replacing uniform inference strategies — the progression from aggregate scaling laws (Singhi, Inference Scaling Laws) through coarse difficulty binning (DiffAdapt's 3 tiers, Snell's 5 quintiles) toward fine-grained continuous allocation (Adaptive Constrained's Lagrangian, our proposed K-d joint optimization) is clear and accelerating. The OSCA k-NN failure and DiffAdapt's 45%-of-oracle ceiling show the field hasn't solved this yet, creating a genuine opportunity window. Second, verification is being elevated from a binary decision (verify or don't) to a multi-dimensional design space with continuous axes: depth (none → ORM → PRM → GenRM), granularity (VG-Search's g parameter), and now potentially per-instance selection across all axes. The proliferation of generative verification variants (GenRM, GenPRM, ThinkPRM, RL^V) has created a rich verification toolkit that no one has organized into a coherent hierarchy with principled selection. Third, the community is demanding deployment-ready online algorithms rather than offline analysis — Snell et al.'s 2048-sample pre-computation was flagged as impractical, while ST-BoN's single-pass design was praised. The winning 2026 papers will combine all three: instance-adaptive, multi-depth verification selection via cheap online signals with formal guarantees. The window for this specific contribution (joint K-d optimization with online routing) is narrow — DiffAdapt (ICLR 2026) and the Adaptive Constrained paper (arXiv Apr 2026) are converging from opposite sides, and a GenPRM team could easily extend to adaptive depth. Speed matters.",

  "meta_advice": "If I were advising a PhD student targeting AAAI 2026 with the proposed K-d joint optimization paper, here is what I would prioritize. First, nail the theoretical contribution before touching GPUs: the bilinear cost decomposition K*(c_g + c_v(d)) admits a clean extension of the Lagrangian framework from the Adaptive Constrained paper (arXiv Apr 2026) — prove that for fixed d, optimal K is determined, reducing the 2D problem to 1D search over d, and derive formal approximation guarantees under verifier accuracy monotonicity. This theoretical backbone is what earns Spotlights (cf. Setlur ICML, DORA NeurIPS). Second, confront the baseline fear head-on in Section 1: run the simplest possible heuristics (problem-length threshold, always-PRM, DiffAdapt's 3-bin approach) and show the gap. If the gap is small, the paper is dead — know this by Week 2, not Week 12. Third, build the difficulty estimator from proven cheap signals: combine ST-BoN's CoE consistency (near-zero cost, already computed during parallel generation) with Oops Wait's entropy/hesitation markers and DiffAdapt's prefill probe, training via imitation learning on Lagrangian oracle labels. This multi-signal estimator is your key empirical contribution and must demonstrably outperform any single signal. Fourth, evaluate on 4+ benchmarks across math (MATH500, AIME 2025), code (LiveCodeBench), and science (GPQA, ScienceQA) — this is table stakes given DiffAdapt's 8-benchmark precedent. Fifth, use DeepSeek-R1-Distill models (MIT-licensed, available at 1.5B-70B) as your generator family, since R1's documented overthinking problem creates the maximum opportunity for adaptive allocation and these models are the community's default evaluation targets. Your compute budget of 200 H100-hours is tight but sufficient if you avoid training large models from scratch — use RL^V's trick of training verifiers from RL rollout data and DiffAdapt's frozen-probe approach to keep training costs minimal. The paper's narrative should be: 'Singhi et al. showed the aggregate-optimal generation-verification ratio; we show this ratio varies dramatically per instance, the variance is predictable from early tokens, and a principled online algorithm exploiting this variance achieves X% accuracy improvement over the aggregate optimum under the same compute budget.' Frame as extending, not contradicting, the seed papers."
}
